# App Traverse coding-agent rules

## Incremental build only

Never, unless the user explicitly asks:

- rebuild, clean, `--clean-first`
- deleting the build directory
- clearing the CMake cache or dependency cache
- re-downloading / rebuilding all dependencies
- `git clean` for the sake of a build

Use only incremental builds of the existing build tree. If the tree is broken, explain the concrete cause. Do not destroy it automatically.

## Fail fast on global invariants

If a global invariant is broken and the app cannot work, fail immediately at
the origin. `assert` is stripped under `NDEBUG`; fatal checks at a real failure
boundary (`RegisterClassW`, `CreateWindowExW`, `CreateEventW`, `PostMessageW`,
`SetEvent`, `DestroyWindow`, `CloseHandle`, `WAIT_FAILED`, required
`LoadApplication`) must use a live check and `abort`. Capture `GetLastError`
immediately on Win32 and pass it to the reused `FatalWin32` helper in the
Windows example. `WriteFatalStderr` writes once: CRT stderr when attached,
otherwise `STD_ERROR_HANDLE`. Do not compile registrar TUs with `NDEBUG` in a
Debug link of aether-objects: `Factory` layout is `#ifndef NDEBUG`. Test names
must match the actual compile configuration; a Debug missing-load test is not
an NDEBUG/Release proof. No modal dialog, retries, or recovery. Do not wrap
the operation in `assert`. Do not duplicate `if (!x)` then `assert(x)`.

Examples: required model/Domain/root failed to load; main native window was not
created; required graph reference or class registration is missing.

- Real state-machine alternative → ordinary `if`
- Globally impossible / fatal state → fail at the origin, then treat the invariant as true
- Do not re-check it in every later function
- Do not write `if (!x) return` / `if (hwnd)` when the previous stage already guaranteed `x`

Before each defensive check: can this state actually be reached after earlier stages succeeded? If not, remove the check.

## Registration: two different things

`APPTRAVERSE_REGISTER` / `EnsureObjectRegistration` registers Æther object
classes. `WNDCLASSW` / `RegisterClassW` registers a native Windows window class.
They are not interchangeable. The native Main class, WndProc, HWND create and
destroy belong to `Win32MainWindowPresenter`. Loading/notify classes belong to
`WinApp::Run` for that process run.

If classes/subsystems must be registered before worker threads: once, at one obvious startup point, before those threads start. Do not call `Ensure...Registration()` on several layers “just in case”. If a call is already guaranteed earlier, do not repeat it below.

## Synchronization

Simplifying helpers must not drop mutex coverage around a condition-variable
predicate. Mutations that waiters observe (`stop`, `has_unread_published`) are
done under the same mutex as `wait`, then `notify`. `ModelSession` has no
platform handles. Publication is reported through the required `on_published`
callback after the mutex is released. Windows signals completion (`SetEvent`)
only after `Run` returns, in the thread lambda, once graph/Domain/storage have
left scope.

## Do not invent entities

Do not add Manager, Service, Coordinator, Context, Runtime wrapper, a registry on top of the existing registry, Lifecycle abstraction, Controller, Repository, helper object, or extra state machine without a real need.

Use App Traverse primitives, aether-objects, platform APIs, locals, RAII, and straight-line control flow. A new type is allowed only if it is a real domain concept, is actually reused, or ownership/lifetime is worse without it. No abstractions “for later”. A small free function is allowed when several call sites share the same operation (for example `FatalWin32`).

## Inline small single-use functions

If a function is small, called from one place, is not a callback/API boundary, and does not hide a genuinely complex operation: inline it and put a short intent comment on the block.

Leave callbacks, WndProc, real algorithms, and reused functions separate. This is not code golf.

## Tests must not shape production

Do not add fields, stage enums, `hold_stage`, raw object addresses, extra atomics, `WasLoaded`/`WasDistilled` getters, test-only hooks, or artificial barriers to production classes for the convenience of tests. Tests check observable production behavior. Keep test infrastructure in test code.

## Distillation is development-only

Distillation / initial-state creation is not production. A load-only/production build only loads persisted state; it does not create state or fall back to distillation. A dev build may, if state is missing: build the initial graph, distill, save, then load via the normal production load path. Dev-only code must be behind a compile-time macro/target and must not appear in the load-only lifecycle path.

## Main-window example startup / shutdown

Startup (not cancelable; user cannot close the app before Main): GUI thread creates Loading → start model thread → model fully loads → initial mirror published → GUI mirror → presenters init → Main created → Loading destroyed.

Shutdown only after full startup: close Main → wake model thread → model frees
graph/Domain/storage on that thread → `Run` returns → Windows `SetEvent` → GUI
join → GUI frees presentation and GUI Domain → exit.

Do not restore close-during-Loading without an explicit assignment.

## Always commit and push

After every finished iteration: commit and push the current working branch. Do not leave finished work local-only. If some tests are blocked, MCP is down, or an external service is unavailable: still commit, push, and say what was not verified.

Do not change `main`, merge, or rewrite published history without an explicit request.

## No hidden refactors

Keep each iteration small and reviewable. Do not “fix” neighboring architecture in passing. If you find a separate problem: record it in `plan.md` / `Progress.md` as TODO and leave it for a later iteration.

## plan.md / Progress.md

Before each task, read `plan.md`, `Progress.md`, and these repository instructions.

- `plan.md`: architecture decisions, next-stage order, deferred work
- `Progress.md`: what was done, commit SHA, test/MCP results, known limitations, push status

Do not mark accepted-by-user yourself.
